1. Create a Local Repository
mkdir DevOpsLab
cd DevOpsLab

git init

Output:

Initialized empty Git repository
2. Create a File
echo "Hello DevOps" > file1.txt

Check status:

git status
3. Add File to Staging Area
git add file1.txt

Check status again:

git status
4. Commit the File
git commit -m "Initial Commit"

Output:

[master (root-commit)] Initial Commit
5. Create GitHub Repository
Login to GitHub.
Click New Repository.
Enter repository name (e.g., DevOpsLab).
Click Create Repository.

You can use the official GitHub website:

GitHub

6. Connect Local Repository with GitHub
git remote add origin https://github.com/username/DevOpsLab.git

Verify:

git remote -v
7. Push Code to GitHub
git branch -M master

git push -u origin master
8. Modification on Master Branch

Modify file:

echo "Modified Content" >> file1.txt

Check changes:

git status

Add modified file:

git add file1.txt

Commit changes:

git commit -m "Modified file on master branch"

Push changes:

git push origin master
9. Fork a Repository
Steps:
Open any public GitHub repository.
Click Fork button at the top-right corner.
GitHub creates a copy in your account.

Example repository:

GitHub Explore Repositories

10. Clone Forked Repository
git clone https://github.com/yourusername/repository-name.git

Move into repository:

cd repository-name
11. Get GitHub Repository Link

Open your repository on GitHub.

Repository URL format:

https://github.com/username/repository-name

Example:

https://github.com/ankitadhagate/DevOpsLab
